# SAMEORIGIN, not DENY. DENY refuses ALL framing including our own, and
# /today is built out of three same-origin iframes — each desk's dataset
# declares `const CLUBS`, so loading three into one document is a
# redeclaration error and a frame is the separate global scope that avoids it.
# With DENY the browser refused those frames, they became opaque origins, and
# reading contentWindow.__data threw "Blocked a frame ... from accessing a
# cross-origin frame", which emptied the whole combined view.
#
# This was invisible in every local test and every guard: `python -m
# http.server` does not send this file, so /today was healthy on localhost and
# broken on the only machine that matters. SAMEORIGIN keeps the clickjacking
# protection that mattered — no other site can frame us — and allows the one
# case the page is built on. frame-ancestors 'self' says the same thing to
# browsers that prefer CSP, and wins where both are understood.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https://cdn.sportmonks.com https://resources.premierleague.com https://media.api-sports.io; connect-src 'self' https://knodunjnsxelmpziupwk.supabase.co; frame-ancestors 'self'

/data/pl_data.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate
